{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial degree polynomial degree","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md","fileName":"Polynomial Degree.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Degree","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Polynomial and Rational Expressions]] · [[Ring and Ideal Foundations]] · [[Polynomial Rings and Factorization]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial and Rational Expressions","Ring and Ideal Foundations","Polynomial Rings and Factorization","Polynomial","Polynomial Long Division","Field","Polynomial Division Algorithm","Leading Coefficient","Sylvester Matrix","algebraessentials.pdf","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial degree polynomial degree","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md","fileName":"Polynomial Degree.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Degree","heading":"Polynomial Degree","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The degree of a nonzero one-variable [[Polynomial]] is the greatest exponent with a nonzero coefficient after like terms have been combined. In (3x^4-2x^3+5), the degree is four.\n\nDegree organizes polynomial terms and controls division. [[Polynomial Long Division]] begins with the highest-degree terms and stops when the remainder has lower degree than the divisor. Multiplying nonzero polynomials adds their degrees, which helps check whether an expansion or factorization has the expected leading structure.\n\nOver a coefficient [[Field]], the same degree comparison underlies the [[Polynomial Division Algorithm]]: a dividend has a unique quotient and a remainder whose degree is smaller than the divisor's. The degree also identifies the [[Leading Coefficient]] and controls the size of a [[Sylvester Matrix]].\n\nThe source uses degree to bound the number of roots over an integral domain and to control quotient and remainder uniqueness over a field. These results connect formal polynomial arithmetic to factorization and extension-field dimension.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial and Rational Expressions","Ring and Ideal Foundations","Polynomial Rings and Factorization","Polynomial","Polynomial Long Division","Field","Polynomial Division Algorithm","Leading Coefficient","Sylvester Matrix","algebraessentials.pdf","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial degree polynomial degree","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Degree.md","fileName":"Polynomial Degree.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Degree","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algebraessentials.pdf]]\n\n[[commutativealgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial and Rational Expressions","Ring and Ideal Foundations","Polynomial Rings and Factorization","Polynomial","Polynomial Long Division","Field","Polynomial Division Algorithm","Leading Coefficient","Sylvester Matrix","algebraessentials.pdf","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}